  "bodyText": "Skip to Main Content\nSKILLS GUIDES \neResources Guide | Skills Guides | Subject Guides\nLibrary | IT Services\nHome\nAcademic & communication skills\nDigital skills\nInformation & research skills\nTraining\nSearch\nNote-taking: a Practical Guide\nNote-taking\nOutline method\nSentence method\nCornell method\nMind mapping\nCharting method\nAnnotation\nNote-taking applications\n\nCornell method\n\nMind mapping\n\nCharting method\n\nMind mapping\n\n\n\nWhat is mind mapping?\n\n\n\nMind mapping is a dynamic and visual note-taking method that engages active participation and critical thinking.\n\nIt involves creating a graphic representation of ideas or principles, showcasing their interrelations.\n\nThis method starts with a central topic and then branches out to connect subtopics, details, and concepts, often enhanced with colours or images for visual appeal.\n\nWhat is mind mapping?Taking notes using mind mappingDigital tools for mind mappingMind mapping exampleAdvantages and disadvantages of mind mappingForthcoming training sessions\n\n\nTaking notes using mind mapping\nBegin with a central theme/idea/topic and branch out into main categories.\nConnect related subtopics to each category, showing the relationship between ideas.\nAdd layers of detail, including facts and examples, to each subtopic.\nUse colours, symbols, and images for a more vivid and memorable map.\nReview your mind map to reinforce knowledge and make connections.\n\n\nDigital tools for mind mapping\n  \n\nThe act of mapping something out is something that's generally much easier with paper and a pen than it is with a mouse and a screen. It's one of those areas where a touch-screen device can come into its own.\n\nFor digital mind mapping, there are many applications you could use. MindGenius is a piece of software available on University computers which can help generate mind maps. But you can also draw mind maps in many note-taking apps and in pretty-much any drawing or image-editing program. You could even use Microsoft PowerPoint.\n\nMindGenius\nMindGenius enables you to capture and visualise ideas and information using a range of 'mind map' layouts\nUsing text and images in PowerPoint and Google Slides\nIncludes a section on diagram tools and drawing features.\n\nAs well as the above, there are many online tools to help you make mind maps. Usually these operate on a 'freemium' model.\n\nDisclaimer: The University does not have a contract with any of these sites. You should always think critically about using third-party applications and never give them any sensitive data.\n\nMindnode\nThis tool provides creative ways to view and enrich mind maps with media, focus mode, and personalisation options.\nZenflowchart\nSimplifies the process of creating flowcharts and mind maps with minimalistic design.\nSimpleMind\nOffers auto layout options alongside the ability to create custom mind maps.\nMindMeister\nFacilitates collaborative mind mapping with history view, presentation mode, and integration with major platforms.\nMindmup\nFree software for creating argument and concept maps, allowing for easy sharing.\nMind mapping example\nAdvantages and disadvantages of mind mapping\n  \nAdvantages\nOffers a visual overview of topics and their interconnections, potentially helping comprehension and recall.\nLogically connects ideas and concepts.\nSupports memory retention through images and colour.\nEfficient for reviewing and reinforcing understanding of material.\nDisadvantages\nLess effective for capturing detailed, linear information like changes from significant points to specific facts.\nCan become complex and unwieldy for dense subjects, risking oversimplification of information.\nDemands high concentration levels and the ability to discern between main ideas and details.\nMay need to be complimented with other note-taking methods for comprehensive coverage.\nCan be time-consuming and the design process may distract from learning the material.\nForthcoming training sessions\n  \n\nForthcoming sessions on Note-taking:\n\nShow details & booking for these sessions\nSTAFF\nRESEARCHERS\nUsing Generative AI at York:\nGoogle NotebookLM\n\n\nTue 3 November, 13:00-14:00, online\nSTAFF\nRESEARCHERS\nUsing Generative AI at York:\nGoogle NotebookLM\n\n\nThu 4 March, 13:00-14:00, online\n\nThere's more training events at:\n\nSkills Guide: Training\nTake a look at our list of events\n<< Previous: Cornell method\nNext: Charting method >>\nLast Updated: Jun 5, 2026 11:01 AM\nURL: https://subjectguides.york.ac.uk/note-taking\n Print Page\nLogin to LibApps\nUniversity Library\nUniversity of York, Heslington, York, YO10 5DD, UK\nTel: +44 (0)1904 323838 | lib-enquiry@york.ac.uk\nAccessibility statement / Legal statements\n© University of York\n\nExcept where otherwise noted, the content on these pages is licensed under CC BY-NC-SA",
